course: dictionary
lesson: 13351
title: Over-the-top words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Hammy is an informal word, so you will hear it in chatty talk more than in formal writing. It means acting in a way that is too big, showy and not natural. Read the clues: «The actor clutched his heart, rolled his eyes and shrieked, I am doomed!» Nobody talks like that at the bus stop, so hammy fits. Ask yourself: is it too much? Then hammy probably fits.
explain: An actor who overacts is sometimes called a «ham». In a school play, «Sam's hammy acting made the audience laugh, but his teacher said, Try to be more natural.» The teacher's words are a clue: hammy means too much, and that is not what she wants.
explain: In everyday talk, «That was a bit hammy!» is usually a mild tease, often funny. The other meaning, tasting or smelling of ham, is a different sense of the same word, so the sentence around it tells you which one to use.
fact: Hammy is an informal word, so it belongs in chatty talk more than in formal writing.
fact: «Hammy» means acting in an over-the-top, exaggerated way that does not seem natural.
fact: A «ham» is an actor who overacts.
fact: «Hammy» can also mean tasting or smelling of ham, as in «The sandwich was hammy and salty.»
fact: The forms are hammy, hammier and hammiest, as in «Her acting was hammier than his.»
exercise: «Her hammy acting made the whole audience giggle.» What does hammy mean here? | Calm and perfectly natural | Old and worn out | *Over-the-top and not natural | Very quiet and shy
exercise: «The sandwich was hammy and salty.» What does hammy mean in this sentence? | Very old | *Tasting of ham | Over-the-top and showy | Cold and wet
exercise: Max says, «That was a bit hammy!» after his friend's silly dance. What is he most likely doing? | Giving a serious warning | Asking for help | Ordering lunch | *Teasing, in a mild and funny way
test: What is a «ham» in a drama lesson? | *An actor who overacts | A kind of food served at lunch | A seat at the back of the theatre | A loud musical instrument
test: Which word means acting in a too-big, showy way? | curt | glum | luminous | *hammy
exam: A teacher says to a pupil in a play, «Try to be less hammy.» What is she telling the pupil? | *The pupil is overacting | The pupil is too quiet and needs to speak up | The pupil is cooking in the wings | The pupil is late for the stage
